#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2331a0df-036e-55aa-adf7-ce24929b4efd" :knowledge-point/title "Manipulating Rational Expressions With Single-Term Numerators"}] :question/id #uuid "465cc593-e81f-5040-bc49-e42b16c0b351" :question/math-academy-id "e-7720" :question/problem "Write $\\dfrac{5x^2}{5x^2-2}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{b}{5x^2-2}$, where $a \\neq 0$. What is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we subtract and add $2$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x^{2}}{5x^{2} - 2} = \\frac{5x^{2} - 2 + 2}{5x^{2} - 2}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5x^{2} - 2 + 2}{5x^{2} - 2} &= \\frac{5x^{2} - 2}{5x^{2} - 2} + \\frac{2}{5x^{2} - 2} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{2}{5x^{2} - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$ and $b=2$, and we get\n\n$$\na + b = 1 + 2 = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48968893-6430-5e1b-ac7a-bd36991f910f" :knowledge-point/title "Manipulating Rational Expressions With Two-Term Numerators"}] :question/id #uuid "0efff2c3-2e5e-5ba3-815d-8419940a5090" :question/math-academy-id "e-7721" :question/problem "Write $\\dfrac{3x^2+5}{3x^2-2x}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{bx+c}{3x^2-2x}$, where $a \\neq 0$. What is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we subtract and add $2x$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x^{2} + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x} = \\frac{3x^{2} - 2x + 2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x^{2} - 2x + 2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x} &= \\frac{3x^{2} - 2x}{3x^{2} - 2x} + \\frac{2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$, $b=2$, and $c=5$, and we get\n\n$$\na + b + c = 1 + 2 + 5 = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81690612-bbae-5d54-a65c-f280d627e051" :knowledge-point/title "Manipulating Rational Expressions With Single-Term Numerators When Factoring Is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "79c2c218-2d4c-5e14-a60e-5f5cba22df4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7722" :question/problem "Write $\\dfrac{3x^3}{x^3+4}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{b}{x^3+4}$, where $a \\neq 0$. What is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we add and subtract $4$ in parentheses in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x^{3}}{x^{3} + 4} &= (3(x^{3} + 4 - 4))/(x^{3} + 4) \\\\\n&= (3(x^{3} + 4) + 3(-4))/(x^{3} + 4) \\\\\n&= (3(x^{3} + 4) - 12)/(x^{3} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3(x^{3} + 4) - 12)/(x^{3} + 4) &= (3(x^{3} + 4))/(x^{3} + 4) + \\frac{-12}{x^{3} + 4} \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{-12}{x^{3} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=3$ and $b=-12$, and we get\n\n$$\na + b = 3 + (-12) = -9\n$$"}]}
